I was on vacation recently to a part of  western Sweden called vadym Skoog where  most of my family on my mother's side is  from and where we own a house that we  usually stay in over the summer now in  this community there is a modest but  beautiful church located right next to  the water in a really Dilek landscape  and this church and its history is  interesting for many different reasons  if you're into that sort of thing at  least but primarily for two reasons one  of them is the very location of the  church itself the building that stands  today doesn't day too far back or was  actually built in 1762 and we built or  renovated in 1802 after much of it had  been tragically destroyed in a fire but  preceding this there have been two  previous churches standing at this very  location the oldest of which dates all  the way back to the 13th century making  it one of the oldest in the region now  what's really interesting is that all  these churches are in the vicinity of a  hill or elevation in the landscape and  this hill is known in Swedish as clock :  which translates to something like the  clock hill or the hill of the clock  excavations at this location has found  that this was a kind of graveyard dating  back to the Iron Age in pre-christian  times graves have been found along with  shards of pottery which points to this  conclusion some of these finds have led  to the general assumption that this Hale  used to be some sort of ritual space or  cultic place for pre-christian religious  traditions the fact that the old church  was built at this very specific location  is very interesting from a historical  point of view it seems to be a case of  Christian appropriation of older  traditions in a strategy to more easily  gain converts if one builds the ritual  space the new ritual space of a new  religion in the same location of the old  tradition this avoids dramatic breaks  with the past and makes transition and  conversion much smoother in fact this  was a very common practice by it many of  the Christian missionaries and one that  was largely very successful the  strategy of taking older ideas places  and holidays that was celebrated or  practiced by the people of these new  regions that one was venturing into and  giving these traditions and new  christian clothing another example of  this relates to the Christian holidays  of All Saints Day and All Souls Day  which at least when coming to Western  Europe began to be celebrated on  November 1st and November 2nd at the  exact same time that the old Celtic  Festival Cerwyn was being held possibly  or I should say probably in a similar  strategy of smoothing out the conversion  now saw witness of course a festival  that is now strongly associated with a  modern holiday of Halloween and in fact  both saw when and also as in Saints Day  and there's sort of syncretism whereas  synthesis is what makes up a lot of what  we associate with Halloween today and I  cover this in a previous video in my  shock Tauber series which you can find  on the channel now the second aspect of  this location that is a very interesting  as of course the old churches themselves  the two older churches one from the 12th  century and the other from the 17th can  still be seen today at least partially  one has found the foundations of these  old buildings so that one can see their  science and general form still today the  12th century church might be one of the  oldest in the entire region of Sweden  and thus is a memory of a time when  Christianity was relatively new here now  this is what I love about studying  history and religion as one can often  imagine what it must have been like for  the people living here and wonder over  what their experience of religion was  the church was placed right next to the  lake and one can picture how people from  different parts of this larger community  arrived perhaps my boats and walked up  these stone steps to the church and what  did the service look like how many  people visited regularly this is 800  years ago and it's almost impossible to  imagine what the world would have  appeared like to them but it is really  exciting to think about and wonder this  is just one church in a small community  and yet it contains so much history so  many people have been here so much has  happened and there's a chumma most every  other place like this it gives us a look  into  the past and a relatively early  Christian community but it also tells us  very interesting things about the very  spread of the religion and how the  transition from pre-christian to  Christian times worked so the next time  you drive by is something like a church  on or building remember that it probably  has a lot of interesting things to tell  you I'll see you next time  [Music]
 

